X:1
T:Galarnad Cwch Enlli
T:The Bardsey Boat Lament
R:cân
S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=28 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung by William Jones (born 1906), a blacksmith, of Gladstone, Aberdaron, Caernarfonshire; recorded (verse 11 only) 7.10.64 (St Fagans tape 820). It was one of the many songs he heard from his grandfather, who lived in Llŷn all his life. The music and words above are verse 11 as recorded; the other verses are sung to the same tune.
N:A lament for the six people drowned when the boat Supply was wrecked on the way from Porth Meudwy to Enlli (Bardsey) on the last day of November 1822; fourteen others reached the shore. The words, printed on a ballad sheet, are by the local poet Evan Pritchard, "Ieuan Lleyn" (1769-1832), of Bryncroes.
N:Where the first note of bars 1 and 10 (the semiquaver) falls on a one-syllable word, it is naturally longer. In bars 4 and 13 the score has a small dotted quaver rest and semiquaver rest after the minim, and in bar 6 a small quaver rest and semiquaver rest after the dotted crotchet, outside the beat: breaths, kept out of the music here. The minim in bar 9 is also given in small notes as c3 c, and the tied B in bar 11 and G in bar 15 as two quavers, for verses with an extra syllable.
